    editorial_html: `<p>Whiteout Survival demonstrates how lower pity thresholds (50-pull vs 100-pull) can actually increase spending frequency rather than decrease extraction. The more achievable pity creates psychological momentum toward "one more pity," enabling higher overall spending than traditional 100-pull systems. This design is potentially more manipulative despite appearing more generous.</p><p>The seasonal competitive leaderboards create artificial spending pressure where players feel socially obligated to maintain ranking through consistent hero acquisition. This compounding effect transforms individual monetization decisions into lifestyle spending commitments.</p><p>The survival gameplay is engaging for strategy enthusiasts, but monetization pressures override core design benefits. The game prioritizes extraction over sustainable player satisfaction.</p><p>Rating: Only recommended for players with strict competitive budgets ($20-30/month absolute maximum). The lower pity threshold is designed to encourage frequent spending rather than genuine generosity. Casual players should avoid entirely; competitive players must maintain firm spending discipline.</p>`
